    ["src/games/updates/994732206.txt"] = [[
0.1.26 | The whole interface now renders on RedzLib, the vendored library in the repo (src/dephubv2lib.lua). The old theme was retired and the shared shell was rewritten as a thin adapter over RedzLib, so every game keeps the same tabs, sections and controls it had before -- look and behaviour did not change, only what draws them. Two things are visible: the open/close key is still N (the shell binds it itself, because RedzLib has no key binding of its own), and group rows now stack their controls vertically instead of side by side (RedzLib has no horizontal container; the controls are all still there, just one under the other). Blox Fruits island teleport also stops disarming by hand: once the character actually reaches the island you picked, that toggle turns itself off. It only flips on real arrival at that flight's destination (25 studs of the island), never starts early and never fires again after you have landed.
0.1.25 | Blox Fruits gains Island Teleport, a new Travel tab. Turn the toggle on, pick an island from the dropdown, and the character flies there. The toggle is a real gate: selecting an island while it is off only remembers the choice, and switching islands while it is on flies again. The destination is the real instance in the map (workspace.Map.<Name>) rather than a hardcoded coordinate, so it does not break when the map changes and it uses exactly the names the user confirmed; if the island is not in the map it says so instead of flying into empty space. The list is the Sea 2 set the user provided: Snow Mountain, Indra Island, Ice Castle, Green Bit, Grave Island, Ghost Ship Interior, Ghost Ship, Forgotten Island, Fishmen, Dressrosa, Darkbeard Arena and Dapper Jack Arena. It has no motor of its own -- it uses the shared teleport by injection, like the fruit feature does. No sliders.
0.1.24 | Blox Fruits fruit detection fixed for the fruits the game spawns. The feature only ever looked at Tools, but a fruit the game spawns into the map is a Model named Fruit/fruit with no Handle -- the Handle (and the ProximityPrompt) only appears once a player touches it. So a spawned fruit never got ESP and the grab never flew, while a fruit dropped by another player (which IS a Tool) worked -- exactly what the user reported. Detection now accepts both shapes: a Tool with OriginalName/Handle, and a Model (or Part) whose name is Fruit-like. ESP also stops orphaning its highlight: the fruit part can change while the fruit exists, and the old code reused the entry and left the adorner pointing at a dead part, which was the glitch the user saw; it now re-targets the adorner when the part changes. The grabbed spawned fruit is reached by flying onto it -- touching it is what makes the game turn the Model into a collectible Tool, so the grab does not refuse just because there is no prompt yet.
0.1.23 | Blox Fruits raid is parked. The raid feature was not behaving the way the user needs and he is going to rebuild it from scratch, so the whole thing was taken out of the active list rather than left half-working: src/games/features/bloxfruits/raid.lua moved to features/bloxfruits/parked/raid.lua (nothing deleted), the RAID section and both toggles (Auto raid, Auto complete raid) are gone from the Farm tab, and the raid is out of the manifest so it no longer loads, instantiates or bundles. The blade hit and the fruit feature are untouched. The backend keeps the combat injection slot in the feature context, reserved for whoever attacks through the shared blade hit next.
0.1.22 | Auto complete raid no longer picks islands by distance -- it walks them by number. The user checked the map with Dex and confirmed the islands are workspace.Map.RaidMap.RaidIsland1, RaidIsland2, RaidIsland3..., so the number IS the order, and "nearest" was the wrong idea: it could fly to an island that is close but out of sequence. Now it starts on island 1, stays while that island still has a live mob, advances to the next number only once it is clear, and when there is no next island AND no mob left alive it calls the round over: it stops acting and just waits, letting the game close the round on its own. It never restarts anything. Mobs are looked for in Workspace.Enemies and Workspace.Characters and inside the current island. Starting the toggle resets the sequence to island 1.
0.1.21 | Blox Fruits raid gets a second, narrower toggle: Auto complete raid. Where Auto raid buys the chip, flies to the tube and starts the raid, Auto complete finishes a raid that is already running -- and does nothing else. It reads the islands under workspace.Map.RaidMap, flies to the one nearest the player right now, lets the blade hit kill the mobs, then recalculates and moves to the next nearest, so it advances one island at a time instead of jumping around. No owner filter: it picks purely by distance, as the user specified. It holds off re-issuing the flight while already close (under 40 studs) so the character does not vibrate in place fighting the tween. The toggle turns the blade hit on when it reaches an island and turns it off again when switched off -- but only if the complete turned it on; a blade hit the player enabled stays enabled.
0.1.20 | Blox Fruits gains its auto raid, built on the two motors the script already has instead of new ones. features/bloxfruits/raid.lua runs the whole cycle the old events.lua did: it makes sure a Microchip is in hand (asking the raid NPC to buy the one for the chosen element when it is not), flies to the raid tube with the shared teleport motor, equips the chip again on arrival and fires the tube button through fireproximityprompt, and once the server says the raid started (the Raids remote's StartTimer event) it starts clearing waves -- by driving the blade hit it was handed, never by firing its own attack, so there is one attack motor in the script, not two. Injection just grew a second piece: every feature now gets {teleport, combat, state}, where combat is the blade hit when it exists; without a teleport the raid refuses outright, and without the blade hit it still enters but reports that it has nothing to attack with. The raid picks its tube by sea (ReplicatedStorage.Util.Realm, the same source the old script used), with the Microchip element selectable from a dropdown. Also: the fruit tab moved under the Farm category -- a fruit picker and a raid runner are both Farm, not two categories for one game.
0.1.19 | Blox Fruits gets its fruit feature, built on the shared teleport motor. features/bloxfruits/fruit.lua covers Grab, ESP and Store: it finds ground fruits (a Tool in Workspace carrying OriginalName and a Handle), highlights them with a distance label, flies to the nearest with the teleport motor and fires its ProximityPrompt, and stores held fruits through the CommF_ remote while respecting a never-store list. The point is the integration the user asked for -- the fruit does NOT roll its own teleport, it receives the motor as a context at creation (options.teleport) and calls tp:GoToInstance(handle); without a motor the ESP and store still work and only the grab refuses. Everything else the old fruits.lua mixed in (gacha, dealer, awakening, chests) stays out. The backend now builds its panel from a per-feature PANELS table (category, tab, section, controls) instead of hardcoding one feature, and passes each feature a context with the teleport motor.
0.1.18 | Blox Fruits gains its teleport motor, and the blade hit loses its sliders. features/bloxfruits/teleport.lua is the open-source fly, ported to the own-module shape with no UI and no toggle: it is infrastructure, not a panel feature. State:Teleport() publishes it to every feature (and the console) already enabled, so a fruit-picker can call tp:GoToInstance(part) or tp:GoToIsland("Jungle") to move the character -- one tested teleport instead of each feature re-implementing its own. Go tweens the HumanoidRootPart with duration scaled by distance, zeroes the linear velocity and noclips during the flight (never Anchors: BF rejects every hit on an anchored root), and carries the full island/boss tables for all three seas. GoToInstance accepts a BasePart or Model, which is the contract a world-scanning feature hands it. The blade hit now exposes only a toggle: range defaults to 300 and the interval to 0.05s -- the strongest that still works -- set on the module (SetRange/SetInterval remain for the console) but with no sliders in the panel.
0.1.17 | Blox Fruits keeps only the blade hit. Every other feature the user took out was moved to features/bloxfruits/parked/ with git mv -- nothing deleted, just out of the active folder -- and parked means parked: not loaded, not instantiated, not in the manifest, not in the bundle (bundle.js now skips that subtree). The global DISABLED flag and its __DEPHUB_BF_DISABLED test override are gone: there is no switch that turns the whole game off, what loads is exactly what is listed in FEATURE_MODULES, and a parked feature is rejected as an unknown name like any other. The backend also lost its now-meaningless parked state from State and GetStatus.
0.1.16 | Blox Fruits is now the user's own script, feature by feature. The backend no longer loads the vendored open-source tree (oss/): it loads one self-contained file per feature from features/bloxfruits/, each doing its own game:GetService and publishing new()/Enable()/Disable() with no DepHub dependency, and it only downloads the tree, builds the panel and flips the toggle. The blade hit comes back first as features/bloxfruits/bladhits.lua: it fires the game's own RegisterAttack/RegisterHit at every enemy in range on a jittered loop, resolves the remotes through Modules.Net with a ReplicatedStorage.Net fallback, refuses cleanly when they are absent, and carries the server-validated hitId as the second remote's 4th argument. Range and interval are set on the module itself (SetRange/SetInterval) rather than through a parallel config on the backend. The oss/ files stay on disk but are no longer tracked or bundled.
0.1.15 | Blox Fruits is turned off -- by request, and nothing was deleted. One master switch (DISABLED = true in src/games/bloxfruits.lua) parks the whole backend: no module initialises, so no feature registers a control, and the frontend is never built, so no tab, section, category or sea category from Blox Fruits appears in the panel. Every file stays committed and downloadable, the loader still routes the two place ids and the backend still returns its State, so the Debug tab reports "BloxFruits.Enabled = no" and explains how to turn it back on. Set DISABLED = false to restore the whole tree exactly as it was.
0.1.14 | Back to Blox Fruits: two features from the old script return. Blade hits auto-swings the equipped weapon at every enemy in range by firing the game's own attack remotes (RegisterAttack + RegisterHit) on a jittered cadence, with sliders for range and interval (ms) and switches for the Enemies/Characters folders and nearby players; the hit carries the server-validated hitId, which it now follows on every capture instead of reusing a stale one. Flashstep (no cooldown) keeps the character's FlashstepCooldown attribute pinned at zero so the move is always ready, and rebinds on respawn. Both sit in the panel (Blade hits in PVP, Flashstep in Player) and clean up fully on unload.
0.1.13 | Fix: the Impulse slider was invisible in Volleyball's Jump feature. A slider shares a line (HStack) with the toggle, and the shell squeezed it to half width -- but the slider's internals are right-anchored with fixed offsets, so at half width its label collapsed and the -/+ buttons overflowed off the edge. Two fixes: the Jump toggle and slider now each take their own full-width line, and the shell's row layout refuses to squeeze a slider (it keeps full width instead of sharing the line).
0.1.12 | Volleyball 4.2 gains its first feature since the restart: Jump Anywhere. One toggle turns on both the jump bypass (patches the game's CharacterActions.Jump check so it stops vetoing jumps) and the extra impulse that speeds the jump up; one slider sets the impulse strength (0-150%, 0% keeps the original jump). With no executor debug.getupvalue/setupvalue the bypass cannot apply, so the toggle reports it and the backend keeps running. It sits in a Movement tab and is fully restored on unload.
